                <pb facs="0679" n="563" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg563"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/4432 Suppl.: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Schurman</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-12-06T12:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 6,
                                1923—12 noon</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>244. Our 243, December 5, 4 p.m. The commander in chief of the U. S. Asiatic
                    Fleet and the commander of the South China <pb facs="0680" n="564"
                        xml:id="pg564"/> Patrol have been instructed by the Navy Department that it
                    is important that the customs should not be seized by Sun Yat-sen. They are
                    ordered to concentrate available ships at Canton for a naval demonstration and
                    also to take necessary measures short of actual warfare. They are to cooperate
                    with other powers for this purpose. The naval commanders are to be guided in
                    general by the advice of the Consulate General at Canton and the Legation at
                    Peking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
